- Thanks, Evan, great to be with you.

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:21.160
- Now before getting started,

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:24.050
I just want to express
my gratitude to you.

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:26.610
I know you've done so much
for the planet, for humanity,

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:29.290
but also for me personally, my family.

00:00:29.290 --> 00:00:32.520
This book was one that
changed not just my life

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:35.502
but my mom's, she bought this
when I was six years old.

00:00:35.502 --> 00:00:36.537
- Oh my gosh.
Still has her phone number

00:00:36.537 --> 00:00:37.860
and her name is in here,

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:40.080
and it really touched her life,

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:41.960
and it was one of the
first books that I read

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:43.890
that was kind of an adult book.

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:45.920
And so, I just want to
thank you for the impact

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:48.570
you've had on me personally and my family.

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:49.770
It really has meant a lot.

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:50.840
- Oh that's very kind of you.

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:52.380
How old are you now, may I ask?

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:53.320
- What year is it?

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:55.040
It's 2018, I'm 38.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:58.170
- Oh my God, since you
were six. (laughing)

00:00:58.170 --> 00:00:59.580
I've been doin' this a little while.

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:01.040
Well thank you, I'm very complimented,

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:02.570
and give my best to your mom.

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:03.770
- I will, thank you.

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:06.440
We're here to talk about
your new book Unshakeable,

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:08.810
and what I found super
interesting about this,

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:11.970
it kind of feels like
it's your life strategies

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:13.961
just applied to the financial markets.

00:01:13.961 --> 00:01:14.970
- [Tony] Yes.

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:16.830
- You open up the first page and it says,

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:18.120
when you're truly unshakeable,

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:20.970
you have unwavering confidence
even amidst a storm,

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:23.660
which is what you've been
teaching mindset-wise forever

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:25.700
but now applied to the financial markets.

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:27.210
Is that a fair assessment?

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:28.720
- Yeah, I think so
except it's really about

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:29.630
the strategies of how to do that.

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:31.610
It's not just about being confident.

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:33.480
What makes you unshakeable
is that you understand

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:36.110
the immutable patterns of the marketplace.

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:39.040
There are patterns just like
seasons that come about.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:42.640
And what happens for
people, humanity transformed

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:45.770
from basically traveling
constantly from place to place.

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:48.120
We built communities once
we understood the seasons

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:49.790
'cause we can know when's
the right time to plant.

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:52.420
Most people do the right
thing at the wrong time.

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:54.040
And if you do the right
thing at the wrong time,

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:57.160
you get no rewards whatsoever.

00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:59.970
In 2008, when I saw the markets
just crashing everywhere,

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:01.600
and I was working with Paul Tudor Jones,

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:02.660
one of the greatest,

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:04.850
top 10 investors in the
history of the world,

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:06.450
I've been working with him for 24 years.

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:08.010
He warned me, he showed me it was coming.

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:10.870
And during that time,
I did incredibly well,

00:02:10.870 --> 00:02:13.310
and I was trying to tell
everybody what's happening,

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:15.480
and I was so angry about
the abuses that happened

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:16.860
that put us in that place.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:18.760
And I thought within six months or a year,

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:19.593
something will be done.

00:02:19.593 --> 00:02:22.180
And two years later, I
saw this documentary.

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:24.810
It was all about how
the meltdown happened,

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:26.080
and I realized nothing had changed.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:28.650
And so, I said, you know
what, I have a unique gift.

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:30.790
I have access to some of the smartest

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:32.020
financial minds on Earth.

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:34.360
What if I interviewed 50
of the smartest people,

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:35.193
put it in a book?

00:02:35.193 --> 00:02:36.670
So I did that over five years,

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:39.720
and I did MONEY Master the
Game, which was 675 pages.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:42.680
Then I wrote this book
because I knew, look,

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:44.710
this is the longest running bull market

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:46.160
we've had in our history now.

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:49.030
We have a correction that's coming.

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:51.920
And most people make stupid
mistakes in a correction

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:52.753
that effect their whole life.

00:02:52.753 --> 00:02:53.840
And I wanted to write a book

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:55.700
that whether you're a
millennial just getting started

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:58.720
or you are a baby boomer and
think you have no time left

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:01.350
to show you how you can
still win in any marketplace.

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:03.280
And the unshakeability comes from the fact

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:04.390
of understanding facts.

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:06.970
So for example, Evan, every single year

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:09.910
we have a correction on
average for the last 100 years.

00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:14.050
A correction is a change
that's less than 20%,

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:15.370
usually 10 to 20%.

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:17.860
The average has been 14% during that time.

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:20.001
Now 14% drop gets people's attention.

00:03:20.001 --> 00:03:22.747
You probably even remember two
years ago in 2016 in January

00:03:22.747 --> 00:03:25.380
we had the worst opening in the history

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:27.880
of the stock market for January,

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:30.420
and we ended up having this
amazing year in the end.

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:31.420
People were freaked out.

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:33.060
I remember they were
reaching out to Davos,

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:35.120
Ray Dalio was there and CNBC went there

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:36.190
and said, what do we do?

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:39.880
The market's lost $2
trillion, now down 9%.

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:41.210
He said, pick up Tony Robbins' book,

00:03:41.210 --> 00:03:43.830
I did and interview and I
shared with him my strategy,

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:46.530
how to make money in any
season, it's called all seasons,

00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:48.960
and the truth of the matter
is, if you practice what he did

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:50.360
you're up 2% at that point.

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:53.100
So, I wanted to write a
book that would show anybody

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:54.610
how to go from where you
are to where you want to be

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:56.470
in the shortest time,
make you unshakeable,

00:03:56.470 --> 00:03:58.730
not based on enthusiasm or confidence,

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:00.540
but based on understanding
the real patterns.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:02.470
One other pattern and then
I'll throw it back to you

00:04:02.470 --> 00:04:06.100
for your question but,
80% of those corrections,

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:08.140
80% never become a bear market.

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:11.770
So the market makes money 77% of the time.

00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:14.290
That means three out of
four years, you're up.

00:04:14.290 --> 00:04:15.250
That's a pretty great deal.

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:17.690
But because of people's fear,
their lack of understanding

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:20.600
of patterns, they overreact
and make dumb decisions.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:25.160
I mean, the market's up
more than 300% since 2009.

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:28.320
If you got on on the worst day of 2009,

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:30.820
you got in on the day of the
crash, you're up 300% today.

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:34.000
But statistics show in
people's 401(k)s for example,

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:36.810
more than 30% of the people
stopped putting money in

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:39.270
back then and 2/3rds of the people reduced

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:41.300
what they put in their
401(k) because of the fear

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:42.790
of what happened 10 years ago.

00:04:42.790 --> 00:04:44.350
So, you got to understand

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:46.200
that you got to be in the marketplace.

00:04:47.226 --> 00:04:49.760
- There's a lot of talk
about control in the book,

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:51.860
what is the one thing that
you want people to understand

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:53.860
about control, when they
feel out of control,

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:55.327
they feel they don't
understand the stock market,

00:04:55.327 --> 00:04:56.830
what's the one message you want people

00:04:56.830 --> 00:04:59.080
to take home around control?

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:01.950
- I think it's that you
have to become an owner,

00:05:01.950 --> 00:05:04.880
and not just a consumer, you
have to take a percentage

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:07.040
of what you earn, as
simplistic as it sounds,

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:10.280
and automate it so it comes
off the top of what you earn.

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:12.220
There's a gentleman that worked for UPS,

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:14.230
his name's Theodore, and Theodore Robinson

00:05:14.230 --> 00:05:18.470
was this guy who never made
more than $14,000 a year

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:21.020
and yet he retired with $70 million

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:22.620
and gave away 35 million
while he was alive.

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:23.570
How did he do it?

00:05:23.570 --> 00:05:26.157
He became an owner, his
best friend said to him,

00:05:26.157 --> 00:05:28.507
"Listen to me, the most
important thing in your life is,

00:05:28.507 --> 00:05:30.210
"we're going to put a wealth tax on you."

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:32.560
He goes, "A wealth tax,
I make 14,000 a year."

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:34.867
He goes, "We're going to make
you wealthy with this tax,

00:05:34.867 --> 00:05:36.997
"it's not a government tax,
it's a tax to yourself.

00:05:36.997 --> 00:05:38.727
"So we're going to take
20% of what you earn,

00:05:38.727 --> 00:05:41.120
"and put it in an investment
account, let it build up."

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:42.270
He said, "I can't afford that."

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:45.150
He goes, "Once it's
automated, you won't notice."

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:48.020
That's all he did that
made him $70 million.

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:49.780
So it's like, people have to understand

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:51.680
that no matter how good
you are in business,

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:53.870
we all need a second
business with no employees,

00:05:53.870 --> 00:05:55.090
and that takes very little time.

00:05:55.090 --> 00:05:56.900
Something that, once you do your homework,

00:05:56.900 --> 00:05:59.110
should take you 15 minutes to an hour

00:05:59.110 --> 00:06:01.180
twice a year to rebalance, perhaps.

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:03.030
But outside of that, that's what this is,

00:06:03.030 --> 00:06:05.370
this is setting up a business
that'll make you do well.

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:09.210
When I was, how old was I,
when I was 24 years old,

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:11.440
25 years old, I worked with
a man named Ken Blanchard,

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:13.380
who wrote those One Minute Manager books.

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:14.480
You probably remember him, Evan.

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:15.380
- [Evan] Yeah, I do.

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:18.460
- Really great guy, and I
helped him take five strokes

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:20.793
off his golf game and
that made me God to him.

00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:23.360
And then he would say, I
want to return the favor.

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:26.730
And he goes, the best advice
I got growing my business was,

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:29.150
I was about to come
out with my first book,

00:06:29.150 --> 00:06:31.540
and he said, a friend of
mine who's very wealthy said,

00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:32.920
do not put that money in your company.

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:35.530
Let the leads go to your company,
do not let the profits go,

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:37.130
and do not spend those profits.

00:06:37.130 --> 00:06:39.670
Put that in a separate account
that nothing ever touches,

00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:42.760
'cause your business will eat
whatever resources you have.

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:44.380
A growing business does that.

00:06:44.380 --> 00:06:45.767
And he said, "Tony, I put that aside,

00:06:45.767 --> 00:06:47.830
"and during the tough times
that's what made me safe."

00:06:47.830 --> 00:06:49.210
Well, I did the same thing with that

00:06:49.210 --> 00:06:51.090
in my original infomercials.

00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:54.430
That alone made me a wealthy
man, so when tough times came,

00:06:54.430 --> 00:06:55.590
I could withstand it.

00:06:55.590 --> 00:06:57.140
So I see so many business owners go,

00:06:57.140 --> 00:06:58.860
yeah, but I have control of my business.

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:01.070
Bullshit, you have influence
over your business.

00:07:01.070 --> 00:07:02.990
Yes you have more influence, but there are

00:07:02.990 --> 00:07:05.250
better entrepreneurs than you
perhaps out there as well.

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:07.900
Or businesses that get greater valuations

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:09.030
than your business will.

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:10.530
And so your ability to grow,

00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:11.730
you want to grow your own business.

00:07:11.730 --> 00:07:14.240
I always tell people, you
want to build two businesses,

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:16.520
the business you're in and
the business you're becoming.

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:18.050
And that you want to have
the business that you're in

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:19.870
and becoming, and an investment business,

00:07:19.870 --> 00:07:21.610
it's really simple once you set it up

00:07:21.610 --> 00:07:23.040
and I think everybody's got to do that

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:24.470
if they really want to
have financial freedom,

00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:26.690
because look, the marketplace changes,

00:07:26.690 --> 00:07:29.550
competition changes, IBM was
the head of the pack forever

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:32.240
and then some little kid
from Microsoft comes along

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:37.240
and buys MS-DOS for $50,000
and then leases it to them,

00:07:37.580 --> 00:07:40.090
and makes themself 3 billion
on that little move alone.

00:07:40.090 --> 00:07:41.930
He didn't even write the software, right?

00:07:41.930 --> 00:07:43.810
But you know, he's doing
great and all of a sudden

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:45.560
this little company comes along,

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:48.380
a couple of guys at Stanford
called Google, you know.

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:51.370
And Apple comes along, so
there's always disruption.

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:53.210
And the disruption could be competition,

00:07:53.210 --> 00:07:55.500
it can be new technology, it
can be a change in the culture.

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:57.870
So even you you do everything
right you could lose.

00:07:57.870 --> 00:07:59.470
I want people to have a back up plan,

00:07:59.470 --> 00:08:01.100
this in the back up plan.

00:08:01.100 --> 00:08:03.110
- I love it, and I want to dive deeper

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:05.372
into some of the tactics,
especially for entrepreneurs.

00:08:05.372 --> 00:08:07.120
But first I had a personal question,

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:09.967
'cause it relates to chaos and control.

00:08:09.967 --> 00:08:13.610
I love chaos, I love it when
the market's out of control,

00:08:13.610 --> 00:08:15.910
it's why I love the stock
market, I love entrepreneurship.

00:08:15.910 --> 00:08:17.790
I think a lot of my
audience can relate to that.

00:08:17.790 --> 00:08:19.910
Where I struggle is actually internal.

00:08:19.910 --> 00:08:21.830
I hate being out of control of my body.

00:08:21.830 --> 00:08:24.070
So I've never been drunk,
I hate roller coasters,

00:08:24.070 --> 00:08:28.630
never done drugs, even
going to your UPW last year,

00:08:28.630 --> 00:08:31.420
I had a hard time fully 100% committing

00:08:31.420 --> 00:08:33.750
because you lose control sometimes.

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:36.890
And I want to fix it, 'cause
I'm coming back in November

00:08:36.890 --> 00:08:38.550
to UPW in New York.

00:08:38.550 --> 00:08:40.060
- I'll see you then, that's awesome.

00:08:40.060 --> 00:08:42.320
- I'm curious about what
your perspective is on that.

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:46.930
- Well, I look at control as a delusion,

00:08:46.930 --> 00:08:48.280
right, that's the first thing.

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:50.800
It's your desire for
the illusion of control

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:53.020
that's pushing you, what
you want is certainty.

00:08:53.020 --> 00:08:54.470
You want certainty you can avoid pain,

00:08:54.470 --> 00:08:55.740
you want certainty you can have pleasure,

00:08:55.740 --> 00:08:57.130
you want certainty you
can have a significant

00:08:57.130 --> 00:08:59.250
and meaningful life,
we all want certainty.

00:08:59.250 --> 00:09:02.220
But, learning ways to
control what you can control

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:04.870
is wonderful, but you
can't control most things.

00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:06.100
What you can do is influence them.

00:09:06.100 --> 00:09:07.460
And so the way I look at it and say is,

00:09:07.460 --> 00:09:09.980
don't give myself the
delusion I have control,

00:09:09.980 --> 00:09:12.180
'cause then I'll feel like
I've lost it at some point.

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:13.530
I never had control, I have influence.

00:09:13.530 --> 00:09:15.420
I can influence my kids,
I can't control them.

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:16.550
I can influence myself,

00:09:16.550 --> 00:09:18.900
I can influence what
happens to me financially,

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:20.420
can't control it completely.

00:09:20.420 --> 00:09:22.560
And so I think if you give
up the illusion of control,

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:24.460
that loosens things up a little bit,

00:09:24.460 --> 00:09:25.430
but I think the second thing is,

00:09:25.430 --> 00:09:26.830
is to focus on what you can control,

00:09:26.830 --> 00:09:29.740
which is what you think,
feel, and what you do.

00:09:29.740 --> 00:09:31.790
And if I focus on the
things I can control,

00:09:31.790 --> 00:09:32.623
I'm going to feel better.

00:09:32.623 --> 00:09:35.890
I mean, all the research
shows that human beings,

00:09:35.890 --> 00:09:38.550
when they feel out of
control, freak out inside.

00:09:38.550 --> 00:09:39.960
And the more they fell they're in control,

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:41.420
the greater their self esteem.

00:09:41.420 --> 00:09:43.530
And the way to have that
additional control is to say,

00:09:43.530 --> 00:09:46.490
okay, what I can control is
what I do within the seasons.

00:09:46.490 --> 00:09:48.080
You can say, I don't like gravity

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:51.400
and step off the side here, but
you're going to pay the price.

00:09:51.400 --> 00:09:53.840
There're certain things that
are outside your control.

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:55.410
But you can use gravity.

00:09:55.410 --> 00:09:56.990
I can fly a helicopter or an airplane

00:09:56.990 --> 00:09:59.310
'cause I understand the
dynamics of how to do that.

00:09:59.310 --> 00:10:01.460
If you understand the
dynamics of the marketplace,

00:10:01.460 --> 00:10:04.280
you use the marketplace, the
marketplace doesn't use you.

00:10:04.280 --> 00:10:05.410
And that's why I wrote Unshakeable.

00:10:05.410 --> 00:10:07.800
I want people to have no more fear,

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:10.400
and when you start understanding
some of these statistics,

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:12.730
like, what's everybody
afraid of, a bear market,

00:10:12.730 --> 00:10:16.730
a bear market is when
market corrects 20% or more.

00:10:16.730 --> 00:10:18.590
Over the last 80 years they've happened

00:10:18.590 --> 00:10:20.650
about every three to five
years, to give you an idea.

00:10:20.650 --> 00:10:22.070
Later years, more five years.

00:10:22.070 --> 00:10:24.220
Well, not we've gone the longest period

00:10:24.220 --> 00:10:25.700
in history without one.

00:10:25.700 --> 00:10:27.380
And yet, people are still afraid.

00:10:27.380 --> 00:10:29.217
After every single bear market we've had

00:10:29.217 --> 00:10:30.970
in the history of the United States,

00:10:30.970 --> 00:10:32.820
it's led immediately into a bull market.

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:36.117
If you remember 2008, 2009, 50% drop.

00:10:36.117 --> 00:10:41.117
In the next 12 months, the S&amp;P was up 69%.

00:10:41.190 --> 00:10:44.030
That's almost 70%, I can
show you every turn down.

00:10:44.030 --> 00:10:46.480
So when you start seeing
that, it's immutable.

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:49.350
It's like winter comes,
but winter isn't forever.

00:10:49.350 --> 00:10:51.423
And winter is a great
time to take advantage

00:10:51.423 --> 00:10:53.340
'cause some people freeze
to death in winter,

00:10:53.340 --> 00:10:55.950
other people ski and
snowboard and get by the fire.

00:10:55.950 --> 00:10:58.580
Well winter, is when things are on sale.

00:10:58.580 --> 00:11:01.260
If somebody said to you,
I got a $400,000 Ferrari

00:11:01.260 --> 00:11:03.660
that you can buy for $70,000,

00:11:03.660 --> 00:11:05.160
you'd probably grab in a heartbeat.

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:06.971
But if somebody takes
you a stock that was $400

00:11:06.971 --> 00:11:08.100
and then 70 bucks, you're like,

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:09.550
oh my god, I can't do this.

00:11:09.550 --> 00:11:11.550
You have to look at the cycles of history.

00:11:11.550 --> 00:11:13.300
And when you do it, you become unshakeable

00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:15.388
and more importantly, you become wealthy.

00:11:15.388 --> 00:11:17.340
- So you talked about Ken Blanchard

00:11:17.340 --> 00:11:19.270
and selling the business and having money,

00:11:19.270 --> 00:11:21.190
and investing it back
into the stock market,

00:11:21.190 --> 00:11:24.060
what would you tell the
start up entrepreneur who,

00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:25.380
maybe is just getting started,

00:11:25.380 --> 00:11:26.770
or maybe still has a full time job,

00:11:26.770 --> 00:11:29.060
and it feels like every
dollar that they're making

00:11:29.060 --> 00:11:31.680
they're just trying to build
their business at the moment,

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:33.290
they haven't cashed out yet.

00:11:33.290 --> 00:11:34.720
Should they be investing
into their business,

00:11:34.720 --> 00:11:36.770
should they still be saving
some to put into the market,

00:11:36.770 --> 00:11:38.260
what would you tell that person?

00:11:38.260 --> 00:11:40.580
- I think you have to put
something outside your business

00:11:40.580 --> 00:11:42.500
because what's the number
one rule of investing

00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:44.120
that even idiots understand?

00:11:44.120 --> 00:11:47.280
Don't put all your eggs
in one basket, right.

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:49.230
And what do 90% of entrepreneurs do?

00:11:49.230 --> 00:11:51.510
Because of the illusion of control,

00:11:51.510 --> 00:11:53.790
they go, I'm going to put
all my money in my business.

00:11:53.790 --> 00:11:55.700
Don't get me wrong,
it's still a great place

00:11:55.700 --> 00:11:57.640
for your money and for your
time and for your energy.

00:11:57.640 --> 00:11:59.410
But you got to take a
piece of what you earn

00:11:59.410 --> 00:12:00.990
and you got to bet on
some other entrepreneurs

00:12:00.990 --> 00:12:03.200
and get outside yourself specifically.

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:04.880
And if you look at, you know,

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:06.993
somebody like Warren
Buffet'll tell you, look,

00:12:06.993 --> 00:12:09.917
he said, "All my wealth is
a result of a couple things,

00:12:09.917 --> 00:12:12.587
"some good genes and compound interest

00:12:12.587 --> 00:12:13.870
"and living in America."

00:12:13.870 --> 00:12:17.250
So, if all you do is get
compound interest on your side,

00:12:17.250 --> 00:12:19.160
the game changes, you don't
have to have a lot of money.

00:12:19.160 --> 00:12:20.430
What Ken Blanchard told me to do,

00:12:20.430 --> 00:12:22.040
that wasn't when I sold the business,

00:12:22.040 --> 00:12:24.740
I was in the business, it
was requiring so much cash

00:12:24.740 --> 00:12:27.040
from me and he was saying, no
matter how much cash you have

00:12:27.040 --> 00:12:27.980
the business will demand it.

00:12:27.980 --> 00:12:30.500
You got to take this
percentage and set it aside

00:12:30.500 --> 00:12:32.100
and not give it to the business.

00:12:32.951 --> 00:12:35.030
I had a situation at one
point where I went through

00:12:35.030 --> 00:12:38.200
a divorce, I initiated it,
but it was a brutal divorce,

00:12:38.200 --> 00:12:40.660
quiet frankly, and when
I married this woman

00:12:40.660 --> 00:12:43.040
she had no money and I
lived in Delmar Castle.

00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:44.642
When I got divorced, she
got an eight times multiple

00:12:44.642 --> 00:12:47.490
on my businesses, and I had
to pay her a million dollars

00:12:47.490 --> 00:12:50.710
a year, for 18 years, and
I was only married to her

00:12:50.710 --> 00:12:53.040
for 14 years, plus $42 million.

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:54.725
So, I can tell you beyond
a shadow of a doubt

00:12:54.725 --> 00:12:57.813
that I woke up every day before
I could feed my own family

00:12:57.813 --> 00:13:00.150
and had to pay this woman a million bucks.

00:13:00.150 --> 00:13:02.160
And I had to pay the taxes on it.

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:04.120
And the only way you can
do that is automate it,

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:06.380
and forget about it, and add value,

00:13:06.380 --> 00:13:08.060
focus on how to add
value in your business.

00:13:08.060 --> 00:13:10.620
So I took it as a tax, off the top,

00:13:10.620 --> 00:13:13.360
I never looked at it again,
and I just automated it,

00:13:13.360 --> 00:13:15.340
and I got to be honest,
the first six to 12 months

00:13:15.340 --> 00:13:18.540
it made me a little crazy,
'cause I'm workin' my guts out,

00:13:18.540 --> 00:13:20.850
and she's not, but what happened was,

00:13:20.850 --> 00:13:22.870
after awhile, my skill increased.

00:13:22.870 --> 00:13:24.710
And what I've found is anybody
who's done this in business,

00:13:24.710 --> 00:13:26.670
even the very beginning and say, look,

00:13:26.670 --> 00:13:27.607
if the government came in right now

00:13:27.607 --> 00:13:30.300
and put a 15% additional
tax on your business,

00:13:30.300 --> 00:13:32.850
you'd scream, you'd yell,
you'd say, I can't pay it,

00:13:32.850 --> 00:13:34.430
and you'd f'in pay it.

00:13:34.430 --> 00:13:35.600
But the difference is,

00:13:35.600 --> 00:13:37.250
the compounding here's what's different.

00:13:37.250 --> 00:13:39.150
I tried to explain compounding like,

00:13:39.150 --> 00:13:41.450
think about if you want
to tell a kid this.

00:13:41.450 --> 00:13:44.180
If you take a 19-year-old kid,

00:13:44.180 --> 00:13:45.440
that's got a little job and you say look,

00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:46.580
I want you, it sounds like a lot,

00:13:46.580 --> 00:13:48.160
but I want you to put aside $300 a month

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:49.500
but let me show you why.

00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:52.470
300 bucks a month set
aside, thrown in the market,

00:13:52.470 --> 00:13:53.853
average market return's been 8%,

00:13:53.853 --> 00:13:58.200
it's been 10% over 100 years,
but most recent 30 years, 8%.

00:13:58.200 --> 00:14:01.720
So, let's say you get the
average return, and it compounds.

00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:03.910
That kid who starts at 19 with 300 a month

00:14:03.910 --> 00:14:07.390
can stop at 27, he's only
done it for eight years,

00:14:07.390 --> 00:14:09.470
never put another dime in,

00:14:09.470 --> 00:14:13.030
and he's only put in 24 or $28,000,

00:14:13.030 --> 00:14:13.997
but that'll grow to 1.8 million

00:14:13.997 --> 00:14:15.557
without putting another dime in.

00:14:15.557 --> 00:14:17.200
That's the power of compounding.

00:14:17.200 --> 00:14:19.680
28 grand, 1.8 million.

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:22.550
If his best friend starts
at 27 when he quits,

00:14:22.550 --> 00:14:25.810
and invests every single
year, all the way to 65,

00:14:25.810 --> 00:14:27.510
and he puts in the same $300 a month,

00:14:27.510 --> 00:14:30.530
he puts in 140 grand, and in that time

00:14:30.530 --> 00:14:33.030
he's got $400,000 less money.

00:14:33.030 --> 00:14:37.240
So, it's not the amount of
money, it's the amount of time.

00:14:37.240 --> 00:14:39.180
Time is your greatest friend here,

00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:40.760
and if you get in the
game, you're going to win.

00:14:40.760 --> 00:14:42.330
An you need to get in the game now,

00:14:42.330 --> 00:14:44.607
not wait 'till your
business is successful.

00:14:44.607 --> 00:14:47.180
- How do you decide what percentage

00:14:47.180 --> 00:14:50.150
you want to allocate to investing?

00:14:50.150 --> 00:14:52.160
- That's a great question, if you look at

00:14:52.160 --> 00:14:54.200
what you want to have in the end,

00:14:54.200 --> 00:14:56.930
you hear very often
financial advisors today

00:14:56.930 --> 00:14:59.010
still use this crazy number, they'll go,

00:14:59.010 --> 00:15:01.130
well you need like, if
you make $100,000 a year,

00:15:01.130 --> 00:15:02.490
you need a million bucks.

00:15:02.490 --> 00:15:04.680
And that's the biggest lie on the planet.

00:15:04.680 --> 00:15:06.630
That was for the days when
you might be able to get

00:15:06.630 --> 00:15:08.840
a 10% return, I don't what
days you could do that

00:15:08.840 --> 00:15:12.410
totally secure, maybe back
when interest rates were 18%.

00:15:12.410 --> 00:15:14.680
But, there's no way that
you're going to get a secure

00:15:14.680 --> 00:15:16.610
environment, the whole idea
is to build your money up,

00:15:16.610 --> 00:15:19.420
build your assets up, and
eventually have the income

00:15:19.420 --> 00:15:20.810
take care of you for
the rest of your life.

00:15:20.810 --> 00:15:22.840
The only reason you invest is for income.

00:15:22.840 --> 00:15:24.900
Income'll buy you your trips,
it'll put your kids through

00:15:24.900 --> 00:15:27.310
college, it'll pay for your
home, it'll give you food.

00:15:27.310 --> 00:15:29.248
People think they want assets,
assets can go up and down

00:15:29.248 --> 00:15:32.020
for any period of time, so
you want put in a secure place

00:15:32.020 --> 00:15:34.920
at some point, where the
income provides all you need

00:15:34.920 --> 00:15:36.070
for the rest of your life.

00:15:36.070 --> 00:15:38.230
Well, pretty hard to do
that if you're putting that

00:15:38.230 --> 00:15:39.483
in an environment where
you're trying to get 10%,

00:15:39.483 --> 00:15:40.540
it's not going to happen.

00:15:40.540 --> 00:15:42.710
So you really need 20 times your income,

00:15:42.710 --> 00:15:43.580
that's really the number

00:15:43.580 --> 00:15:44.943
that most people should be utilizing,

00:15:44.943 --> 00:15:46.820
which doesn't sound very compelling.

00:15:46.820 --> 00:15:49.700
But when you understand
compounding like $300 a month

00:15:49.700 --> 00:15:51.790
for eight years, turns into 1.8 million,

00:15:51.790 --> 00:15:53.350
never put another dime
in, you start to go,

00:15:53.350 --> 00:15:55.800
hm, maybe I can still win this game.

00:15:55.800 --> 00:15:59.770
We don't think geometrically,
we think step at a time.

00:15:59.770 --> 00:16:02.600
And the way stocks work, or
the way any investment works,

00:16:02.600 --> 00:16:04.150
there's compounding, and the compounding

00:16:04.150 --> 00:16:05.850
is the greatest gift that we have.

00:16:06.960 --> 00:16:10.147
- So I guess it depends
on your age for how much

00:16:10.147 --> 00:16:11.130
percentage of your money--
- Well to specifically

00:16:11.130 --> 00:16:13.130
answer your question, I would
do as much as you could,

00:16:13.130 --> 00:16:15.650
but I would say 10% would be the minimum,

00:16:15.650 --> 00:16:17.190
and you ideally want to build to 15 or 20.

00:16:17.190 --> 00:16:19.680
And they did a study for people who said

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:23.260
they had no ability to invest,
they had no money whatsoever,

00:16:23.260 --> 00:16:26.460
and it was done by this
group of professors

00:16:26.460 --> 00:16:28.890
that were eventually
nominated for a Nobel Prize.

00:16:28.890 --> 00:16:33.170
And what they found was, if
they got them to put aside 3%,

00:16:33.170 --> 00:16:37.350
and anyone can do 3%, and
then with every additional

00:16:37.350 --> 00:16:40.380
increase in salary, they
put another 3% aside,

00:16:40.380 --> 00:16:43.863
within 15 years, the average
person was saving 15%.

00:16:43.863 --> 00:16:46.310
65% were saving 19%.

00:16:46.310 --> 00:16:48.680
These are workers in middle class America,

00:16:48.680 --> 00:16:51.970
making $35,000 a year,
to give you an idea.

00:16:51.970 --> 00:16:55.020
And at 19 or 20% you become wealthy.

00:16:55.020 --> 00:16:58.180
You'd have to screw up to
not make it at that point.

00:16:58.180 --> 00:16:59.910
But if you think about
it, today most people

00:16:59.910 --> 00:17:03.510
are going to retire, based
on actuarial statistics

00:17:03.510 --> 00:17:06.780
for no less than 20 years,
some of them 30 or 35 years,

00:17:06.780 --> 00:17:08.337
well they only worked for 40 years.

00:17:08.337 --> 00:17:09.920
And most people did
not set aside the money

00:17:09.920 --> 00:17:12.110
to make that happen, so
that's why I wrote this book.

00:17:12.110 --> 00:17:14.630
I want to do something so
simple you can read in a weekend

00:17:14.630 --> 00:17:16.780
and say, okay, here's
where I'm being screwed

00:17:16.780 --> 00:17:18.800
and I'm going to save
all that money alone.

00:17:18.800 --> 00:17:21.660
Just the savings you can make
on not overpaying in fees,

00:17:21.660 --> 00:17:24.380
every 1% in fees you pay
more than you should,

00:17:24.380 --> 00:17:27.150
is 10 years of income you
give up in retirement.

00:17:27.150 --> 00:17:28.290
Because they're compounding.

00:17:28.290 --> 00:17:30.750
We know that compounding
works on appreciation,

00:17:30.750 --> 00:17:32.740
but compounding also works on fees.

00:17:32.740 --> 00:17:33.810
So you got to understand that.

00:17:33.810 --> 00:17:35.090
In Unshakeable I walk you through it.

00:17:35.090 --> 00:17:36.820
'Cause you cannot believe the abuses

00:17:36.820 --> 00:17:38.650
that happen in this system.

00:17:38.650 --> 00:17:41.274
- You talk a lot about the
house and how you're bettin'

00:17:41.274 --> 00:17:44.100
against the house and the
house always makes money.

00:17:44.100 --> 00:17:45.620
So if somebody's now listening to you

00:17:45.620 --> 00:17:48.330
and they say, okay
great, 10, 15%, 20 maybe,

00:17:48.330 --> 00:17:50.270
I'm going to save, what's my next step?

00:17:50.270 --> 00:17:52.038
I don't get the stock market,
I don't know what to do next,

00:17:52.038 --> 00:17:54.090
I'm not going to give it to a mutual fund.

00:17:54.090 --> 00:17:54.970
What's my next step?

00:17:54.970 --> 00:17:56.410
- Well I'm glad to hear you
say you weren't going to

00:17:56.410 --> 00:17:58.500
give it to a mutual fund
because most people don't know

00:17:58.500 --> 00:18:02.010
that 98% of all mutual
funds in any 10 year period

00:18:02.010 --> 00:18:03.410
do not even match the market.

00:18:03.410 --> 00:18:06.100
So if you're going to buy
something in that area

00:18:06.100 --> 00:18:06.933
and you're going to start out simple,

00:18:06.933 --> 00:18:09.970
you want to buy the Index
because it's so cheap.

00:18:09.970 --> 00:18:10.803
But you got to think about,

00:18:10.803 --> 00:18:12.790
where do most people put their money?

00:18:12.790 --> 00:18:16.160
They put it in a 401(k),
that's where 90% of Americans

00:18:16.160 --> 00:18:17.850
have the largest sum of
money, it's their home

00:18:17.850 --> 00:18:19.920
and their 401(k) and the 401(k) is bigger.

00:18:19.920 --> 00:18:21.770
And the problem is, like you said,

00:18:21.770 --> 00:18:22.603
people don't know where to go.

00:18:22.603 --> 00:18:24.210
So what I tell people first step,

00:18:24.210 --> 00:18:26.793
make the most important
financial decision in your life,

00:18:26.793 --> 00:18:27.943
that you're going to pay yourself first,

00:18:27.943 --> 00:18:30.260
so there's going to be some money
you're going to keep forever

00:18:30.260 --> 00:18:32.580
for you and your family,
not give it to Kate Spade,

00:18:32.580 --> 00:18:34.470
or to Amazon or anybody else.

00:18:34.470 --> 00:18:36.160
Here's that percentage and automate it,

00:18:36.160 --> 00:18:36.993
so you don't see it.

00:18:36.993 --> 00:18:39.640
Second step is, now we
got this bucket of money

00:18:39.640 --> 00:18:41.237
we're building up, where
the hell do I put it?

00:18:41.237 --> 00:18:44.440
And in that case, the most
important financial decision,

00:18:44.440 --> 00:18:47.030
investment decision, I
should say, is the decision

00:18:47.030 --> 00:18:49.610
of asset allocation,
which is a big word for,

00:18:49.610 --> 00:18:51.900
how much money do you put
at low risk environments,

00:18:51.900 --> 00:18:54.580
where you probably get smaller
returns, but less risk?

00:18:54.580 --> 00:18:56.770
How much do you put in
higher risk environments,

00:18:56.770 --> 00:18:58.430
like the stock market or real estate,

00:18:58.430 --> 00:19:00.330
where you have unlimited
upside but you also have

00:19:00.330 --> 00:19:02.050
unlimited downside as well?

00:19:02.050 --> 00:19:03.640
And so how do you balance that out?

00:19:03.640 --> 00:19:04.690
And the answer to the question,

00:19:04.690 --> 00:19:06.790
we walk you through in the book
how to make those decisions,

00:19:06.790 --> 00:19:08.350
but here are some of the criteria.

00:19:08.350 --> 00:19:09.910
Your age, as you said, is number one

00:19:09.910 --> 00:19:11.840
because how much time do you need

00:19:11.840 --> 00:19:13.740
before you need this money is huge.

00:19:13.740 --> 00:19:16.590
If I'm in a position, and
let's say I got two buckets,

00:19:16.590 --> 00:19:20.099
a secure bucket, low risk,
you know, low returns,

00:19:20.099 --> 00:19:22.700
a risk growth bucket, more risk,

00:19:22.700 --> 00:19:24.810
more growth, or more loss.

00:19:24.810 --> 00:19:28.420
What percentage, do I put
like 60% secure, 40% here,

00:19:28.420 --> 00:19:31.940
do I do, 20% secure, 80% at risk?

00:19:31.940 --> 00:19:33.210
And that has to do with number one,

00:19:33.210 --> 00:19:34.070
how much time do you need?

00:19:34.070 --> 00:19:35.630
So, if you need the money in five years,

00:19:35.630 --> 00:19:36.830
you can't afford to take giant risks,

00:19:36.830 --> 00:19:38.270
'cause if you screw up, you got no room.

00:19:38.270 --> 00:19:40.630
If you're 20 years old,
hell, you can screw up

00:19:40.630 --> 00:19:42.380
many times and still win the game.

00:19:42.380 --> 00:19:43.213
So, that's number one.

00:19:43.213 --> 00:19:45.310
Number two is, what's your risk tolerance?

00:19:45.310 --> 00:19:47.130
And in the book we have
some tests for you,

00:19:47.130 --> 00:19:48.837
'cause what people think
their risk tolerance is

00:19:48.837 --> 00:19:50.690
and what it really is
are two different things.

00:19:50.690 --> 00:19:53.180
We play a game at one of
my seminars where I say,

00:19:53.180 --> 00:19:54.540
I put some music on and say,

00:19:54.540 --> 00:19:55.880
make change with people around you.

00:19:55.880 --> 00:19:56.713
And they go, what?

00:19:56.713 --> 00:19:58.010
I go, make change, so they
reach in their pockets

00:19:58.010 --> 00:20:00.030
and start making change.

00:20:00.030 --> 00:20:02.440
And it ends after one
song, four or five minutes

00:20:02.440 --> 00:20:03.840
and I go on to the next subject

00:20:03.840 --> 00:20:05.280
and inevitably somebody
stands up and goes,

00:20:05.280 --> 00:20:07.860
I want my money back, this f'er over here

00:20:07.860 --> 00:20:10.880
took a hundred bucks
and gave me five bucks.

00:20:10.880 --> 00:20:13.127
And I go first of all, what
makes you think it's your money?

00:20:13.127 --> 00:20:14.050
And I said, second of all,

00:20:14.050 --> 00:20:16.200
what makes you think the game is over?

00:20:16.200 --> 00:20:18.410
And if somebody's stressed
about a hundred bucks,

00:20:18.410 --> 00:20:20.500
and you're going to be an investor,
you're going to be in trouble.

00:20:20.500 --> 00:20:21.730
So you got to know your risk tolerance.

00:20:21.730 --> 00:20:24.460
And then last, what's
your access to cash flow?

00:20:24.460 --> 00:20:26.070
If you have a lot of
extra access cash flow,

00:20:26.070 --> 00:20:27.550
you've got room to take more risks.

00:20:27.550 --> 00:20:29.350
If you don't have much cash flow,

00:20:29.350 --> 00:20:30.660
you're going to have to be more selective

00:20:30.660 --> 00:20:33.020
in what you're going to put at risk.

00:20:33.020 --> 00:20:36.160
- How important is the
education side in that,

00:20:36.160 --> 00:20:38.470
should I just be putting
my money into the Index,

00:20:38.470 --> 00:20:39.400
and I don't have to worry about it,

00:20:39.400 --> 00:20:41.030
it's going to go up seven to 10% a year,

00:20:41.030 --> 00:20:44.880
and pay super low fees,
versus, I need to understand

00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:48.440
the actual stocks to buy, if
Amazon's a good buy or not.

00:20:48.440 --> 00:20:51.400
How much do you advise
understanding the individual

00:20:51.400 --> 00:20:53.870
companies, or should it just
be kind of set it, forget it,

00:20:53.870 --> 00:20:55.360
buy the Index, look at it once a year,

00:20:55.360 --> 00:20:56.560
then don't worry about it?

00:20:56.560 --> 00:20:58.030
- I don't think you want
to just do the Index

00:20:58.030 --> 00:20:59.817
because you want to have
different asset classes.

00:20:59.817 --> 00:21:02.150
If you look at the best
investors in the world,

00:21:02.150 --> 00:21:04.840
they all understand that
they're going to be wrong.

00:21:04.840 --> 00:21:07.170
You talk to Ray Dalio, the
greatest hedge fund investor

00:21:07.170 --> 00:21:10.040
in the history of the world,
guy made a fortune in 2008

00:21:10.040 --> 00:21:11.700
when everyone else was losing money,

00:21:11.700 --> 00:21:12.533
and what does he tell you?

00:21:12.533 --> 00:21:14.127
He says, "Tony, you have to have money

00:21:14.127 --> 00:21:15.837
"in each different asset class because

00:21:15.837 --> 00:21:17.353
"different environments, the
environments will change,

00:21:17.353 --> 00:21:19.357
"no matter how smart
people think they are,

00:21:19.357 --> 00:21:20.377
"they're going to be wrong.

00:21:20.377 --> 00:21:21.677
"And you got to structure yourself

00:21:21.677 --> 00:21:23.890
"so you're able to make
money no matter what."

00:21:23.890 --> 00:21:26.670
So you don't want just the Index.

00:21:26.670 --> 00:21:29.240
But, what you do want to do, is
you do not want to be probably

00:21:29.240 --> 00:21:31.840
somebody who's trying to
evaluate individual stocks.

00:21:31.840 --> 00:21:34.590
That's really somebody who's
going to try and be a trader,

00:21:34.590 --> 00:21:36.070
and in most cases that's
not going to work.

00:21:36.070 --> 00:21:37.800
Now if you're Warren Buffet,
that's a different piece.

00:21:37.800 --> 00:21:39.470
But it may be better to
invest with Warren Buffet,

00:21:39.470 --> 00:21:41.180
who's better at selection than you are,

00:21:41.180 --> 00:21:42.520
than you trying to do it.

00:21:42.520 --> 00:21:44.900
Stock picking, nobody wins at it.

00:21:44.900 --> 00:21:46.010
The best in the world don't win.

00:21:46.010 --> 00:21:47.250
You got people that are suppose to be

00:21:47.250 --> 00:21:48.720
the best hedge fund guys on Earth,

00:21:48.720 --> 00:21:51.160
and what's their track record
over the last seven years?

00:21:51.160 --> 00:21:53.930
It's pathetic, they've not
even matched the market.

00:21:53.930 --> 00:21:55.240
And so that's why you've
seen this shake up

00:21:55.240 --> 00:21:56.690
in the hedge fund industry.

00:21:56.690 --> 00:21:58.260
'Cause after you pay for fees,

00:21:58.260 --> 00:22:00.160
even with their brilliant minds,

00:22:00.160 --> 00:22:02.670
you end up lower than if you
just own the market itself.

00:22:02.670 --> 00:22:05.020
So the answer to your question
is you need to diversify.

00:22:05.020 --> 00:22:07.080
Let me give you the four things if I may,

00:22:07.080 --> 00:22:09.181
that are the four most
important things that I found,

00:22:09.181 --> 00:22:10.990
they were the only things in common,

00:22:10.990 --> 00:22:12.640
I interviewed 50 of the
smartest financial people

00:22:12.640 --> 00:22:14.800
in the world but they all
have different approaches.

00:22:14.800 --> 00:22:17.040
You look at Warren Buffet, he
has a very different approach

00:22:17.040 --> 00:22:19.060
than say, Ray Dalio does, right?

00:22:19.060 --> 00:22:21.120
One's looking at trends in the market,

00:22:21.120 --> 00:22:22.640
the other's looking at
individual companies

00:22:22.640 --> 00:22:24.564
and trying to take advantage
of the best possible

00:22:24.564 --> 00:22:27.140
valuations that he possibly can.

00:22:27.140 --> 00:22:28.850
But here's what they all
agree on, number one,

00:22:28.850 --> 00:22:30.500
and I know this sounds basic,

00:22:30.500 --> 00:22:32.830
they're obsessed with not losing money.

00:22:32.830 --> 00:22:35.360
Most investors are obsessed
with trying to make money.

00:22:35.360 --> 00:22:38.754
But because they know
that if I lose $100,000,

00:22:38.754 --> 00:22:43.040
and I want to grow it back,
I can't just go and grow

00:22:43.040 --> 00:22:44.740
at a small rate to make that happen.

00:22:44.740 --> 00:22:48.140
Let's say I lose 50%
of it, I have $50,000.

00:22:48.140 --> 00:22:49.760
How much do I got to grow to get even?

00:22:49.760 --> 00:22:52.330
Most people say, well you
lost 50%, you got to grow 50%.

00:22:52.330 --> 00:22:54.130
They don't realize, if you had $100,000,

00:22:54.130 --> 00:22:57.020
it's only worth 50 now, you
got to grow 100% to get even.

00:22:57.020 --> 00:23:00.180
So the smartest investors know
they're going to be wrong,

00:23:00.180 --> 00:23:01.640
and they have an asset allocation,

00:23:01.640 --> 00:23:03.240
they've broken up their assets in a way

00:23:03.240 --> 00:23:04.210
that even if they're wrong,

00:23:04.210 --> 00:23:05.930
it's not going to hurt them severely.

00:23:05.930 --> 00:23:08.390
Second, this is the one
that's more interesting,

00:23:08.390 --> 00:23:11.930
they all are obsessed with this idea of,

00:23:11.930 --> 00:23:14.170
how do I have the least amount of risk,

00:23:14.170 --> 00:23:16.040
with the most amount of return?

00:23:16.040 --> 00:23:18.850
They call it asymmetrical risk reward.

00:23:18.850 --> 00:23:20.100
And what they look at is,

00:23:20.100 --> 00:23:21.590
when I started working
with Paul Tudor Jones,

00:23:21.590 --> 00:23:23.980
one of the 10 best investors in history,

00:23:23.980 --> 00:23:25.870
he had lost money for some time.

00:23:25.870 --> 00:23:28.463
He had been the most successful investor,

00:23:28.463 --> 00:23:30.410
and the meltdown that happened, you know,

00:23:30.410 --> 00:23:32.560
the largest stock market
in history meltdown

00:23:32.560 --> 00:23:35.330
that we had, where you lost 20% in a day,

00:23:35.330 --> 00:23:37.890
he made 200% that year for his guys,

00:23:37.890 --> 00:23:40.700
and made I think, if I
remember right, 40% that month.

00:23:40.700 --> 00:23:42.480
I mean, this is a
brilliant, brilliant guy,

00:23:42.480 --> 00:23:44.230
but here's how he does it.

00:23:44.230 --> 00:23:45.830
He doesn't invest like most investors.

00:23:45.830 --> 00:23:48.100
He doesn't say like, where
can I get a nice return?

00:23:48.100 --> 00:23:50.090
He's lookin' for a five to one.

00:23:50.090 --> 00:23:52.970
Where if he risks a dollar,
he believes he'll make five.

00:23:52.970 --> 00:23:54.780
Now he's going to be wrong, he
knows he's going to be wrong.

00:23:54.780 --> 00:23:57.090
So if I risk a dollar, try
to make five and I'm wrong,

00:23:57.090 --> 00:23:58.700
okay, I can risk another dollar.

00:23:58.700 --> 00:24:02.610
He can risk four out of five
dollars and still make money.

00:24:02.610 --> 00:24:05.333
So, that puts you in a
very different position

00:24:05.333 --> 00:24:06.980
than most people.

00:24:06.980 --> 00:24:09.910
If you look at somebody like,
Richard Branson's a friend

00:24:09.910 --> 00:24:12.770
and Richard is just a genius as you know,

00:24:12.770 --> 00:24:13.980
but what most people don't have a clue

00:24:13.980 --> 00:24:15.870
is when he started Virgin Airlines,

00:24:15.870 --> 00:24:17.870
he only started with five airplanes,

00:24:17.870 --> 00:24:20.630
giant investments, right,
these Boeing business jets.

00:24:20.630 --> 00:24:22.830
But what people don't know is
he spent a year negotiating

00:24:22.830 --> 00:24:24.130
so he had no downside.

00:24:24.130 --> 00:24:26.200
If he didn't succeed in
the first three years,

00:24:26.200 --> 00:24:28.040
he negotiated a deal
to give back the planes

00:24:28.040 --> 00:24:30.690
with no credit lost, nothing lost to him.

00:24:30.690 --> 00:24:32.790
So it was all upside, and no downside,

00:24:32.790 --> 00:24:35.400
that's called asymmetrical risk reward.

00:24:35.400 --> 00:24:36.980
Kyle Bass is a friend of mine.

00:24:36.980 --> 00:24:41.010
He is well known during
the 2008 crisis because

00:24:41.010 --> 00:24:45.060
he took $20 million and
turned it into four billion,

00:24:45.060 --> 00:24:48.490
in the worst economic environment
we've had in 80 years.

00:24:48.490 --> 00:24:49.323
And how'd he do it?

00:24:49.323 --> 00:24:50.450
Well I could explain the details,

00:24:50.450 --> 00:24:52.060
but he bet against real estate

00:24:52.060 --> 00:24:53.530
when everybody else was betting on it,

00:24:53.530 --> 00:24:54.670
he looked at the facts.

00:24:54.670 --> 00:24:59.210
But here's the secret, every
time he invested six cents,

00:24:59.210 --> 00:25:02.860
and in order to risk six
cents, his upside was a dollar.

00:25:02.860 --> 00:25:05.790
So if you risk six cents,
you can be wrong 15 times

00:25:05.790 --> 00:25:06.623
and still make money.

00:25:06.623 --> 00:25:08.630
Well he wasn't wrong
15 times, thus he made

00:25:08.630 --> 00:25:11.460
two billion out of 20
million in 3 1/2 years.

00:25:11.460 --> 00:25:14.560
So, asymmetrical risk reward
is what they're looking for.

00:25:14.560 --> 00:25:16.640
You can't always get a five
to one, a three to one,

00:25:16.640 --> 00:25:18.490
but you want to be looking
for it, and you want to

00:25:18.490 --> 00:25:20.200
think differently than the average person.

00:25:20.200 --> 00:25:25.200
Third, they're all completely
obsessed with tax efficiency,

00:25:25.200 --> 00:25:27.620
but not until they first
have done the first two.

00:25:27.620 --> 00:25:29.410
If you just try to do
something based on taxes,

00:25:29.410 --> 00:25:30.860
it's not the right investment.

00:25:30.860 --> 00:25:33.640
But if it's something where
you can feel relatively certain

00:25:33.640 --> 00:25:35.580
you won't lose money, and you've set up

00:25:35.580 --> 00:25:38.280
the asset allocation that if
you're wrong you're still okay,

00:25:38.280 --> 00:25:40.340
and then you've got
tremendous risk reward,

00:25:40.340 --> 00:25:43.490
small risk, big reward,
then you want to make sure

00:25:43.490 --> 00:25:44.533
it's tax efficient, 'cause as
I've trained all the people

00:25:44.533 --> 00:25:46.710
that work for me, don't
tell me the return,

00:25:46.710 --> 00:25:49.290
tell me the net return,
tell me after taxes,

00:25:49.290 --> 00:25:51.420
after fees, after everything,

00:25:51.420 --> 00:25:53.210
'cause it's very different
than what people do.

00:25:53.210 --> 00:25:56.277
And so, if you take a dollar,
and you know compounding,

00:25:56.277 --> 00:26:00.290
and you double it 20 times,
it's a million and 48,000.

00:26:00.290 --> 00:26:02.210
But if you just paid 33% in tax,

00:26:02.210 --> 00:26:03.880
which is less than most
of what your viewers

00:26:03.880 --> 00:26:05.930
probably are, I should say more than what,

00:26:05.930 --> 00:26:07.688
less than probably what most
of your viewers or listeners

00:26:07.688 --> 00:26:09.410
probably pay, most would probably be

00:26:09.410 --> 00:26:10.800
more in the 50% tax range.

00:26:10.800 --> 00:26:14.600
And you think about
it, that 33% taken out,

00:26:14.600 --> 00:26:17.550
after 20 turns, you think
well, how much is that,

00:26:17.550 --> 00:26:21.050
it was a million and 48, you take out 33%,

00:26:21.050 --> 00:26:24.217
well that's only 330,000,
roughly, around 300 grand.

00:26:24.217 --> 00:26:27.190
No, no, no, that math doesn't
work, this is compounding.

00:26:27.190 --> 00:26:30.410
Instead of a million and
48,000, if you pay 33% tax

00:26:30.410 --> 00:26:34.480
each year, you have $28,000,
instead of a million and 48.

00:26:34.480 --> 00:26:36.490
So tax efficiency baby, is huge.

00:26:36.490 --> 00:26:38.850
And then the last one is,
what I talked to you about

00:26:38.850 --> 00:26:41.250
from the beginning,
which is, every investor

00:26:41.250 --> 00:26:45.230
believes in diversification
across multiple elements.

00:26:45.230 --> 00:26:47.630
In other words, you don't
want to just buy real estate,

00:26:47.630 --> 00:26:49.740
you want different
assets, or just the Index,

00:26:49.740 --> 00:26:51.430
like you described, that's a disaster.

00:26:51.430 --> 00:26:53.270
'Cause there'll be times
when the Index will get hit,

00:26:53.270 --> 00:26:54.790
and you need something to balance it out.

00:26:54.790 --> 00:26:56.266
If you're only in one place
you're going to get hurt,

00:26:56.266 --> 00:26:58.710
and the pain will be so
strong you'll probably sell

00:26:58.710 --> 00:27:00.860
and then have a permanent
wound or a permanent problem.

00:27:00.860 --> 00:27:02.150
You don't want that to happen.

00:27:02.150 --> 00:27:04.220
So you need diversity
across different assets.

00:27:04.220 --> 00:27:06.740
You don't want just one
giant piece of real estate.

00:27:06.740 --> 00:27:08.740
You want diversity within asset classes,

00:27:08.740 --> 00:27:09.995
so multiple pieces of real estate,

00:27:09.995 --> 00:27:12.830
or owning the Index as the example.

00:27:12.830 --> 00:27:15.690
You want diversification
across different economies,

00:27:15.690 --> 00:27:17.680
and you want diversification across time,

00:27:17.680 --> 00:27:19.510
which is what dollar
cost averaging can do.

00:27:19.510 --> 00:27:22.720
So, those are four things
that the greatest investors

00:27:22.720 --> 00:27:24.030
on Earth all do in common,

00:27:24.030 --> 00:27:26.970
even though they have different
approaches to making money.

